principal_claims.statement_tex:
\label{prop:multiplier}
If \eqref{eq:target} has a solution at $(m,t)$ and $s$ is any positive
odd integer, it has a solution at $(m,st)$.

\label{prop:residual}
The matrix $C$ is positive semidefinite, has rank $r$, satisfies
$C^2=nC$, and has diagonal entries $r$. Moreover $P$ completes to a
Hadamard matrix if and only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:factor}
 C=B^{\mathsf T}B\quad\hbox{for some }B\in\{\pm1\}^{r\times n}.
\end{equation}
For even $n$, all off-diagonal entries of $C$ have parity $r$.

For any rational row space $V\subseteq\mathbb Q^N$, exactly one of
the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $V\cap\mathbb Z^N$ contains a vector with odd coordinate sum;
\item $V^\perp\cap\mathbb Z^N$ contains a vector all of whose
coordinates are odd.
\end{enumerate}
For a partial Hadamard row space, the first case supplies a zero-budget
witness. The second case supplies arithmetic parity feasibility,
but does not assert a sign vector exists.
